   418	\section{The Klein Quartic}\label{sec:kleinquartic}
   419	
   420	In this section, we prove Statement $(**)$ in Lemma \ref{lemma:twoconditions}, focusing on the Klein quartic. This is the smooth plane curve $C$ given by the equation
   421	\[x^3y + y^3z + z^3 x=0.\]
   422	This classical curve is very well studied, see e.g.~\cite{zbMATH01574981} for a survey. We will show that there is a theta characteristic $\etasym$ on $C$ such that $(C,\eta)$ are adjoint to $336$ heptagons, and that the map $\operatorname{adj}$ is unramified at each of these heptagons. We achieve this by using the large automorphism group $\operatorname{Aut}(C) \cong \mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{F}_7)$ of $168$ elements. Notice that the automorphism group acts linearly on $C$, i.e.~as a subgroup of $\mathrm{PGL}_3(\mathbb{C})$. This is because the plane model of $C$ is precisely its canonical embedding.
   423	
   424	We make use of the fact that among the $36$ even theta characteristics of $C$ there is exactly one that is invariant under all automorphisms (see \cite{zbMATH05874806}), which we call $\etasym$. In the plane model, it can be represented as \[\etasym + H \sim 2(e_1 + e_2 + e_3)\] where $e_1 = [1,0,0], e_2=[0,1,0], e_3=[0,0,1]$. The points $e_1$, $e_2$, $e_3$ are inflection points of the Klein quartic: We have $H \sim 3 e_1 + e_3 \sim 3 e_3 + e_2 \sim 3 e_2 + e_1$, so that, for example, $\etasym \sim 2e_1 + e_2 - e_3$.
   425	
   426	\begin{theorem}\label{KleinQuarticThetaHeptagon}
   427		There is a heptagon in $\P^2$ whose adjoint is the Klein quartic $C$ and such that the associated theta characteristic is $\etasym$, the unique even theta characteristic of $C$ invariant under all automorphisms of $C$.
   428	\end{theorem}
   429	
   430	
   431	The construction of an associated heptagon uses the symmetries of $C$ as follows. Let $\zeta \in \C$ be a primitive seventh root of unity and consider the 7-fold symmetry $\varphi \in \operatorname{Aut}(C)$ given by 
   432	\[ \varphi : [x,y,z] \longmapsto [\zeta^4 x, \zeta^2 y, \zeta z] \] 
   433	For any point $p \in \P^2$, write $p_i = \varphi^{i-1}(p)$ for $i=1,\dots,7$. 
   434	
   435	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:heptagonKlein}
   436	Let $p_1\in C\setminus \{e_1,e_2,e_3\}$ be a point such that the lines $\ol{p_1 p_3}$ and $\ol{p_5 p_7}$ intersect in a point on the curve $C$. Then the heptagon given by the lines
   437	\[ L_1 = \ol{p_6p_1},\,\, L_2 = \ol{p_7p_2}, \,\, L_3 = \ol{p_1p_3}, \,\, L_4 = \ol{p_2p_4}, \,\, L_5 = \ol{p_3p_5}, \,\, L_6 = \ol{p_4p_6}, \,\, L_7 = \ol{p_5p_7} \]
   438	has the curve $C$ as adjoint.	
   439	\end{lemma}
   440	\begin{proof}
   441		We show that the $14$ points $p_{ij}$ for $j\neq i-1,i+1$ lie on the Klein quartic. These points form two orbits under the symmetry $\varphi$ of order $7$ of the Klein quartic, namely $p_1, \ldots,p_7$ and the orbit of the intersection point of $L_3$ and $L_7$, which lies on $C$ by assumption.
   442	\end{proof} 
   443	
   444	\begin{proof}[{Proof of \Cref{KleinQuarticThetaHeptagon}}]
   445		We compute the intersection point $\ol{p_1p_3}\cap \ol{p_5p_7}$ as follows. If we write $q = \lambda_1 p_1 + \mu_1 p_3 = \lambda_2 p_5 + \mu_2 p_7$ with $\lambda_i,\mu_i\in \C$, then $\mu_2 p_7 = \lambda_1 p_1 + \mu_1 p_3 - \lambda_2 p_5$. So, with points written as column vectors, we have 
   446		\[ q=\begin{pmatrix}
   447		p & \varphi^2(p) & \mathbf{0} 
   448		\end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}
   449		p & \varphi^2(p) & \varphi^4(p) 
   450		\end{pmatrix}^{-1} \cdot \varphi^6(p).
   451		\]
   452		We want this intersection point $q$ to lie on $C$, as well. The map $\psi\colon \P^2 \to \P^2$,
   453		\[ p \mapsto \begin{pmatrix}
   454		p & \varphi^2(p) & \mathbf{0} 
   455		\end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix}
   456		p & \varphi^2(p) & \varphi^4(p) 
   457		\end{pmatrix}^{-1} \cdot \varphi^6(p) \]
   458		is given in coordinates by 
   459		\[ p \longmapsto 
   460		\begin{pmatrix} (\zeta^4+2\zeta^2-2\zeta-\zeta^6)x \\ 
   461		(\zeta^2+2\zeta-2\zeta^4-\zeta^3)y \\
   462		(\zeta+2\zeta^4-2\zeta^2-\zeta^5)z \end{pmatrix}.
   463		\]
   464		In particular, this shows that the map is well-defined everywhere, also at the points where the matrix $(p \,\, \varphi^2(p) \,\, \varphi^4(p))$ is not invertible.
   465		The candidate points that can give rise to heptagons with adjoint $C$ are the points in $C\cap \psi^{-1}(C)$. The curve $\psi^{-1}(C)$ is a plane quartic curve given by an equation
   466		\[ g = a_1 x^3 y + a_2 y^3 z+ a_3 z^3 x \]
   467		where $a_1$, $a_2$, and $a_3$ are pairwise distinct, non-zero complex numbers (see the following \Cref{example:KleinHeptagon} for details). In terms of these coefficients, we can express the intersection $C\cap \psi^{-1}(C)$ as the divisor 
   468		\[ 3(e_1 + e_2 + e_3) + R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + R_4 + R_5 + R_6 + R_7 \]
   469		on $C$, with coordinates $R_i = [b \cdot \zeta^3 \cdot \alpha_i^3, \alpha_i,1]$ where $b = (a_1 - a_2)/(a_3-a_1)$ and $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_7$ are the seven roots of
   470		\[ t^7  = - \frac{b+1}{b^3}.\]
   471		Note that the intersection points $R_i$ form one orbit under the symmetry $\varphi$, as they should. By the construction of Lemma \ref{lemma:heptagonKlein}, the orbit of points $R_1, R_2, \ldots, R_7$ gives rise to a heptagon with adjoint $C$.
   472		
   473		To compute the associated theta characteristic on $C$, we go back to \Cref{lem:thetachar}.
   474		The theta characteristic is determined by the sum of the residual points. By construction, these are $R_1,R_2,\ldots,R_7$, and the points $Q_i = \psi(R_i)$. So we need to show that 
   475		\[ \sum_{i=1}^7 R_i + \sum_{j=1}^7 Q_j = 3H + \etasym. \]
   476		This follows from the fact that $\psi^{-1}(C).C = 3(e_1 + e_2 + e_3) + \sum_{i=1}^7 R_i$, which also implies that $\psi(C).C = 3(e_1 + e_2 + e_3) + \sum_{j=1}^7 Q_j$. Adding these two relations shows
   477		\[ 8 H \sim 6(e_1 + e_2 + e_3) + \sum_{i=1}^7 R_i + \sum_{j=1}^7 Q_j \sim 3 (H + \etasym) + \sum_{i=1}^7 R_i + \sum_{j=1}^7 Q_j, \]
   478		which simplifies to $3H + \etasym = \sum_{i=1}^7 R_i + \sum_{j=1}^7 Q_j$ as desired.
   479	\end{proof}
   480	
   481	\begin{example}\label{example:KleinHeptagon}
   482		We can produce an explicit heptagon whose adjoint is the Klein quartic following the proof above: Symbolic computation yields $g=f\left(\psi(x,y,z)\right)=a_1 x^3 y + a_2 y^3 z+ a_3 z^3 x$, where
   483		\begin{align*}
   484			a_1 &= -7 \left(1+\zeta\right)\left(2-11 \zeta+6 \zeta^2-\zeta^3-4 \zeta^4+9 \zeta^5\right)\\
   485			a_2 &= 7 \left(1+\zeta\right)\left(-2-3 \zeta+8 \zeta^2+\zeta^3-10 \zeta^4+5 \zeta^5\right)\\
   486			a_3 &= 7 \left(1+\zeta\right)\left(-2-3 \zeta-6 \zeta^2+\zeta^3+4 \zeta^4+5 \zeta^5\right).
   487		\end{align*}
   488		The points $R_1,\dots,R_7$ are given by $R_i=[b\cdot\zeta^3,\alpha_i^3,\alpha_i,1]$, where 
   489		$\alpha_i$ is a root of $t^7+(b+1)/b^3$ and $b=(a_1-a_2)/(a_3-a_1)$ is a root of the irreducible cubic
   490		\[
   491		   t^3+t^2-2t-1.	
   492		\]
   493		The labelled lines are then given as
   494		\[ L_1 = \ol{R_6R_1},\,\, L_2 = \ol{R_7R_2}, \,\, L_3 = \ol{R_1R_3}, \,\, L_4 = \ol{R_2R_4}, \,\, L_5 = \ol{R_3R_5}, \,\, L_6 = \ol{R_4R_6}, \,\, L_7 = \ol{R_5R_7} 
   495		\]
   496		by Lemma \ref{lemma:heptagonKlein}.
   497	
   498		We will also need to know that the adjoint map is unramified at this point. To see this, we rewrite the adjoint in the form
   499		\[
   500		   	{\rm adj}\colon \left\{\begin{array}{ccc}
   501				|\sO_{\P^2}(H)|^7 & \to & |\sO_{\P^2}(4H)|\\
   502				(L_1,\cdots,L_7) & \mapsto & \sum_{i=2}^6 \det(L_1|L_i|L_{i+1})\cdot\prod_{j\neq 1,i,i+1} L_j
   503			\end{array}\right.
   504		\]
   505		where, by abuse of notation, the $3\times 3$-matrices $(L_1|L_i|L_{i+1})$ on the right are formed by taking the coefficients of the lines $L_1,\dots,L_7$ as column vectors. This formula is well known can be verified directly by evaluating at the residual points of the line arrangement, see also \cite{zbMATH00989549}*{Section~3.2} and \cite{KRzbMATH07286075}*{Formula~(1.1)}. Note also that the expression on the right is multilinear and hence compatible with rescaling of $L_1,\dots,L_7$.
   506		We compute the Jacobian of this map, viewed as a map $(\C^3)^7\to\C^{15}$, at the heptagon above. It is a $15\times 21$-matrix, which is indeed of rank $15$. We verified through symbolic computation that the $15\times 15$-minor with columns indexed by $1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 10, 11, 13, 14, 16, 17, 19, 20$ does not vanish.\hfill$\Diamond$
   507	\end{example}
   508	
   509	Since the automorphism group of the Klein quartic $C$ is a subgroup of $\mathrm{PGL}_3(\C)$, it acts linearly on $\P^2$ and therefore induces an action on heptagons. Concretely, if $\sigma \in \operatorname{Aut}(C)$, it acts on a heptagon $(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7)$ by sending $L_i$ to the line $\sigma(L_i)$. This action preserves incidences on $C$ meaning that if $L_i\cap L_j\subset C$, then $\sigma(L_i)\cap \sigma(L_j)\subset C$ as well. Therefore, any automorphism of $C$ maps a heptagon with adjoint $C$ to another heptagon whose adjoint is $C$. For the set of residual points, we have $\sigma.(\mathcal{R}(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7)) = \mathcal{R}(\sigma.(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7))$. 
   510	
   511	\begin{theorem}
   512		(1) The automorphism group ${\rm Aut}(C)$ of the Klein quartic acts freely on the orbit of the heptagon $(L_1,\dots,L_7)$ constructed in Example \ref{example:KleinHeptagon}. The symmetry $\varphi$ of order $7$ acts by cyclic permutation, hence there are $168/7=24$ elements modulo cyclic permutation. 
   513	
   514		(2) The fiber of the adjoint map over the Klein quartic contains the union of two orbits as in (1), namely the orbits of $(L_1,\dots,L_7)$ and of $(L_7,\dots,L_1)$.
   515	\end{theorem}
   516	
   517	\begin{proof}
   518		By construction of $L_1,\dots,L_7$, the symmetry $\varphi$ of order $7$ acts by cyclic permutation of the labels. To show that ${\rm Aut}(C)$ acts freely, it suffices to prove that no symmetry of order 2 or 3 stabilizes the heptagon $(L_1,\dots,L_7)$. Assume to the contrary that $\sigma \colon \P^2 \to \P^2$ is a symmetry of order $2$ or $3$ that stabilizes the heptagon.
   519		
   520		Since $\sigma$ stabilizes the heptagon, it induces an element of the dihedral group $D_7$ acting on $(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7)$. This immediately implies that $\sigma$ cannot have order $3$ because $D_7$ has order $14$. Any element $\sigma\in D_7$ of order $2$ stabilizes an element. We may assume that $\sigma(L_1) = L_1$, so that $\sigma$ is the permutation $(7\; 2) (6\; 3) (5\; 4)$.
   521		Recall that the $7$-fold symmetry $\varphi$ is the cyclic permutation by the construction in \Cref{KleinQuarticThetaHeptagon}, so that
   522		\begin{align*}
   523		(1\; 2\; 3\; 4\; 5\; 6\; 7) (7\; 2) (6\; 3) (5\; 4) = (7\; 2) (6\; 3) (5\; 4) (1\; 2\; 3\; 4\; 5\; 6\; 7)^6
   524		\end{align*}
   525		shows $\varphi \sigma = \sigma \varphi^6$. This shows that $\sigma$ normalizes the cyclic subgroup $\langle \varphi \rangle$.
   526		Since the 3-fold symmetry $[x,y,z] \mapsto [z,x,y]$ is also in the normalizer of $\langle\varphi\rangle$, the cardinality of this normalizer is at least $2\cdot3\cdot7$. This is a contradiction to \cite{zbMATH01574981}*{\S2.1}, which says that there are eight $7$-Sylow subgroups in the symmetry group of $C$.
   527		
   528		Now let $\sigma$ be any symmetry of $C$. Since the sum of the residual points $\mathcal{R}(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7)$ is $3H + \etasym$, we get that the sum of $\mathcal{R}(\sigma.(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7))$ is $\sigma.(3H + \etasym)$, which is $3H + \etasym$. So the theta characteristic associated with the heptagon $\sigma.(L_1,L_2,\ldots,L_7)$ is also $\etasym$.
   529	\end{proof}
   530	
   531	We conclude by restating our main result.
   532	\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:main}
   533	    A general pair $(C,\eta)$ of a plane quartic curve $C$ and an even theta characteristic $\eta$ arises from precisely $14\times 24=336$ heptagons. A general plane quartic $C$ hence arises from $14\times (24\times 36)=14\times 864$ heptagons.  
   534	\end{theorem}
   535	
   536	\begin{proof}
