   259	\section{Intersection calculus on products of curves}\label{sec:intersection}
   260	We will compute the number of points in $T_7$ via intersection products on the space $C^7$. First, we recall some notions on the intersection product on Cartesian products.
   261	
   262	\subsection{Cartesian Products of Curves}
   263	Let $C$ be a smooth complete curve of genus $g$. We collect some results on the intersection product on the cartesian product $C^n$, using \cite{ACGH} and \cite{Bastetal} as our main references. For indices $1\leq i_1< i_2< \dots< i_k\leq n$, we denote by \[\operatorname{pr}_{i_1,\ldots,i_k}\colon C^n \to C^k\] the projection onto the components indexed by $i_1,\dots,i_k$. We will consider the ring $N(C^n)$ of cycles up to numerical equivalence. In particular, we will focus on the subring of $N(C^n)$ generated by the following two types of divisors (usually called \emph{tautological ring of cycles}). We write $x_i$ for the class $\pr_i^*(x)$, where $1\leq i \leq n$ and $x$ is any point of $C$, and $\Delta_{ij}$ for the class $\operatorname{pr}_{ij}^*\Delta$, where $\Delta\subseteq C\times C$ is the diagonal. We furthermore denote by $\Delta_{12\dots n}$ the small diagonal $\Delta = \{(x,x,\dots,x) \,|\, x\in C \} \subseteq C^n$, which is also the intersection of the divisors $\Delta_{12},\Delta_{23},\ldots,\Delta_{n-1,n}$.
   264	The next result collects the relations among these classes.
   265	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:basicrelations}
   266		Let $C$ be a smooth curve of genus $g$. The following equalities hold in $N(C^n)$.
   267		\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
   268			\item $x_1\cdot x_2 \cdot \dots \cdot x_n = 1$.
   269			\item $x_i^2 = 0$,  $x_i\cdot \Delta_{ij} = x_i\cdot x_j$  and $\Delta_{ij}^2 = -(2g-2)\cdot x_i\cdot x_j$.
   270			\item $\Delta_{12\dots n} \cdot \Delta_{ij} = -(2g-2)$. 
   271		\end{enumerate}
   272	\end{lemma}
   273	\begin{proof}
   274		(1) The divisors $\pr_i^*(x_i)$ for $i=1,\ldots,n$ intersect transversally in the unique point $(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n)$. 
   275		
   276		(2) This is because the first relation holds in $C$ and the last two hold in $C\times C$. We have $x_1\cdot x_2=1$ because of (1) and $x_i \cdot \Delta = 1$ because the divisors $\pr_i^*(x_i)$ and $\Delta$ intersect transversally in the unique point $\{(x_i,x_i)\}$. To show that $\Delta^2 = -(2g-2)$ holds on $C\times C$, consider the diagonal embedding $j\colon C \to C^2, x\mapsto (x,x)$. By the adjunction formula, we have $j^*\Delta \sim K_C - j^*(K_{C^2})$. Since $j^*(K_{C^2}) = j^*(K_C\boxtimes K_C) = 2K_C$ we conclude $j^*\Delta \sim -K_C$, which has degree $-(2g-2)$.
   277		
   278		(3) The small diagonal is the image of the embedding $j\colon C \to C^n, x\mapsto (x,x,\dots,x)$. By definition, the intersection number is $\deg j^*\Delta_{ij} = \deg j^*\operatorname{pr}_{ij}^*\Delta$, so we can just look at the composed map $\operatorname{pr}_{ij}\circ j$, which is the usual diagonal embedding in $C\times C$. So the intersection number is the same as $\Delta^2$ on $C\times C$, which is $-(2g-2)$. 
   279	\end{proof} 
   280	
   281	In general, the relations in Lemma \ref{lemma:basicrelations} show that any monomial in the classes $x_i$ and $\Delta_{ij}$ can be written in reduced, square free form
   282	\begin{equation}\label{eq:monomialform} 
   283	x_{i_1}\cdot x_{i_2} \cdot \dots \cdot x_{i_r} \cdot \Delta_{h_1k_1} \cdot \dots \Delta_{h_sk_s} 
   284	\end{equation}
   285	where $i_1,\dots,i_r$ and $(h_1k_1),\dots,(h_sk_s)$ are pairwise distinct and the two groups of indices are disjoint, i.e.~
   286	\[ \{ i_1,\dots,i_r \} \cap \{ h_1,k_1,\dotsc,h_s,k_s \} = \emptyset. \]
   287	To determine the degree of this class, assuming it is $0$-dimensional, it is enough to compute $\Delta_{h_1k_1} \cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_sk_s}$. 
   288	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:graphrelation}
   289		To compute $\Delta_{h_1k_1} \cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_sk_s}$, define the graph $\Gamma$ on the vertex set $V=\{h_1,k_1,\dots,h_s,k_s \}$ where two vertices are connected by an edge if and only if they appear as one of the indexes in $\Delta_{h_1k_1} \cdot \dotsc \cdot\Delta_{h_sk_s}$. 
   290		Assume that this graph $\Gamma$ is connected.
   291		\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
   292			\item The graph $\Gamma$ has precisely $s$ edges.
   293			\item If $\Gamma$ has more than $s$ vertices, then it is a tree on $s+1$ vertices and $\Delta_{h_1k_1}\cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_sk_s}$ is the class of a small diagonal in $C^{s+1}$.
   294			\item If $\Gamma$ has $s$ vertices, then $\Delta_{h_1k_1}\cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_sk_s} = -(2g-2) \in N(C^s)$.
   295			\item If $\Gamma$ has less than $s$ vertices, then $\Delta_{h_1k_1}\cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_sk_s} = 0$.
   296			\item If $\Gamma$ is not connected, then the product $\Delta_{h_1k_1} \cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_sk_s}$ can be computed as a product over the different connected components of $\Gamma$
   297		\end{enumerate}
   298	    
   299	\end{lemma}
   300	\begin{proof}
   301		(1) This is clear by construction.
   302		
   303		(2)  If $\Gamma$ is connected but has more than $s$ vertices, then it is a tree with $s+1$ vertices. We show the claim by induction on $s$. In the base case $s=1$, we have the usual diagonal in $C^2$. The induction step of adding one leaf and the corresponding edge to the tree is \Cref{lemma:basicrelations}(3).
   304		
   305		(3) If $\Gamma$ has $s$ vertices and $s$ edges, then it has a spanning tree with $s-1$ edges. Up to relabeling, we may assume that this corresponds to $\Delta_{h_1,k_1}\cdot \dotsc \cdot \Delta_{h_{s-1}k_{s-1}}$. By (1), this is the class of a small diagonal in $C^s$, and the intersection with the last diagonal $\Delta_{h_s,k_s}$ is computed in Lemma \ref{lemma:basicrelations} to be $-(2g-2)$.
   306		
   307		(4) In this case, we are intersecting $s$ divisor classes in the space $C^k$ for some $k<s$, so we obtain $0$.
   308		
   309		(5) This is true by construction of the graph $\Gamma$. 
   310	\end{proof}
   311	
   312	
   313	\subsection{The class of the Scorza correspondence}
   314	
   315	Let now $C$ be a smooth plane quartic curve and $\eta$ an even theta characteristic such that $h^0(C,\eta)=0$. We consider the Scorza correspondence $S(\eta)\subseteq C\times C$.  According to \cite{Dolgachev}*{Equation (5.37)}, this is linearly equivalent to  $S(\eta) \sim \pr_1^*\eta + \pr_2^*\eta + \Delta$, hence its class in $N(C^2)$ is
   316	\begin{equation}\label{eq:scorzaclass} 
   317	[S(\eta)] = 2x_1+2x_2+\Delta. 
   318	\end{equation}
   319	
   320	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:scorzanef}
   321		The class of the Scorza correspondence is nef, meaning that $S(\eta) \cdot D \geq 0$ for every curve $D\subseteq  C^2$.
   322	\end{lemma}
   323	\begin{proof}
   324		Let $D\subseteq C^2$ be an irreducible curve. If $D$ is not contained in $S(\eta)$, then $S(\eta)\cdot D \geq 0$. If instead $D\subseteq S(\eta)$, then in particular $D\cap \Delta = \emptyset$, thus
   325		\[ D\cdot S(\eta) = D\cdot (2x_1+2x_2+\Delta) = 2D\cdot (x_1+x_2) \]
   326		The class $x_1+x_2$ is ample on $C\times C$ because it is the class of the ample line bundle $\pr_1^*\mathcal{O}_C(x_1)\otimes \pr_2^*\mathcal{O}_C(x_2)$. Hence $D\cdot (x_1+x_2) >0$ and $D\cdot S(\eta) >0$, as well. 
   327	\end{proof}
   328	
   329	Now we consider on $C^n$, as before, the pullbacks $S_{ij} = \pr_{ij}^*S(\eta)$ and the intersection $T_n = S_{12}\cap S_{23}\cap \dots \cap S_{(n-1)n} \cap S_{1n}$. This is an intersection of $n$ divisors in a space of dimension $n$, so we expect it to have dimension $0$. In this case we can compute the number of points  via the product of the classes of the $S_{ij}$ in $N(C^n)$. Actually, in Proposition \ref{prop:reconstructionscorza} we just need to bound the number of isolated points in $T_7$, and this can be done even without showing that the whole set is finite because $S(\eta)$ is nef.
   330	
   331	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:scorzabound}
   332		For every $n\geq 2$, let $S_{123\dots n} = S_{12}\cap S_{23}\cap \dots \cap S_{n-1,n}$, so that $T_n = S_{1234\dots n}\cap S_{1n}$. The following holds:
   333		\begin{itemize}
   334			\item[(1)] The intersection $S_{123\dots n}$ is of pure dimension $1$, as expected.
   335			\item[(2)] The number of isolated points (counted with multiplicity) in $T_n$ is bounded by the intersection product $S_{12}\cdot \ldots \cdot S_{(n-1)n}\cdot S_{1n}$. 
   336		\end{itemize}
   337	\end{lemma}
   338	\begin{proof}
   339			(1) We proceed by induction on $n$. If $n=2$, then $S_{12}$ is the Scorza correspondence in $C^2$, which is a curve. Now consider the projection $\pr_{12\ldots n-1}\colon S_{12\ldots n} \to C^{n-1}$ which forgets the last component. The image of this map is contained in $S_{12\dots n-1}$, which has pure dimension $1$ by the induction hypothesis. To conclude, it is enough to show that the map has finite fibers. However, the fiber of this map over $(x_1,\ldots,x_{n-1})$ is identified with the subset $\{x_n \in C \,|\, h^0(C,\eta+x_{n-1}-x_n)>0 \}$. This corresponds to the unique effective divisor in $|\eta+x_{n-1}|$, which has degree three. Recall that $h^0(C,\eta+x_{n-1})=1$ because $h^0(C,\eta)=0$.
   340	
   341			(2) Write the curve $S_{123\ldots n} = D_1\cup \dots \cup D_r$ as a union of irreducible curves. The isolated points of $T_n$ are given by the intersection of $S_{1n}$ with the $D_i$ that are not contained in $S_{1n}$. Up to relabeling, we can assume that these are $D_1,\dots,D_h$.
   342			If we write the class $[S_{123\dots n}] = m_1D_1+\dots+m_hD_h$, the number of isolated points of $T_n$, counted with multiplicity, is $\sum_{i=1}^h m_i \cdot (D_i\cdot S_{1n})$. Since the class $S_{1n}$ is the pullback of a nef class, it is nef (see \cite{Laz}*{Example 1.4.4.(i)}), so that $S_{1n}\cdot D_i \geq 0$ for all $i=h+1,\dots, r$. Hence the number of isolated points is less than or equal to the intersection product $S_{1234\dots n}\cdot S_{1n}$. Since $S_{123\dots n}$ has the expected dimension, its class is given by $S_{12}\cdot \ldots \cdot S_{(n-1)n}$ and we are done.
   343	\end{proof}
   344	
   345	Next, we compute the relevant intersection products.
   346	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:scorzaclassmany}
   347		For any $n\geq 2$, the equality
   348		\[ S_{12}\cdot \ldots \cdot S_{(n-1)n} \cdot S_{1n} = 2\cdot 3^n-6 \]
   349	    holds in $N(C^n)$. In particular, $S_{12}\cdot \dots \cdot S_{67} \cdot S_{17} = 4368$ in $N(C^7)$.
   350	\end{lemma}
   351	\begin{proof}
   352		Using \eqref{eq:scorzaclass}, we can write
   353		\[ S_{12}\cdot \ldots \cdot S_{(n-1)n} \cdot S_{1n} = \prod_{i=1}^n (2x_i + 2x_{i+1}+\Delta_{i,i+1}) = \sum a_1\dots a_n \]
   354		where in the product we consider the indices modulo $n$ and the last sum is over all possible choices of $a_i \in \{2x_i,2x_{i+1},\Delta_{i,i+1}\}$. Consider now a product $a_1\dots a_n$ and assume that it is nonzero. Assume also that $a_1=2x_2$. Since the product is nonzero, we see that $a_2 \ne 2x_2$ so that $a_2=2x_3$ or $a_2 = \Delta_{2,3}$. Hence $a_1a_2 = 2c_2\cdot x_2x_3$, where $c_2=2$ if $a_2=2x_3$ and $c_2 = 1$ if $a_2 = \Delta_{2,3}$. So we can rewrite $a_1\dots a_n = 2\cdot c_2 \cdot  x_2x_3 \cdot a_3\dots a_n$. Again, because this class is nonzero, we must have $a_3\ne 2x_3$ and we can repeat the previous reasoning. This way we see that
   355		\[  a_1\dots a_n = 2^k (x_1\dots x_n) = 2^k, \qquad \text{ where } k = |\{i \in \{1,\dots, n \} \,|\, a_i\ne \Delta_{i,i+1} \}|. \]
   356		An analogous reasoning shows that the same holds if $a_1 = 2x_{1}$. With this in mind, fix a subset $I\subseteq \{1,\dots,n\}$ with $I\ne \emptyset$. We claim that, amongst the products $a_1\dots a_n$ such that $a_i \ne \Delta_{i,i+1}$ if and only if $i\in I$, there are precisely two that are nonzero and that these are both equal to $2^{|I|}$. Indeed, up to a cyclic permutation, we may assume that $1\in I$, so that $a_1=2x_1$ or $a_1=2x_2$. If $a_1\dots a_n\ne 0$, the previous computation shows that the other $a_i$ are uniquely determined by the value of $a_1$ and that the product is $a_1\dots a_n = 2^{|I|}$. This proves our claim. 
   357	
   358		We are left to consider the product $\Delta_{12}\cdot \Delta_{23} \cdot \ldots\cdot \Delta_{1n}$. Since the genus of $C$ is $3$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:graphrelation} shows that this is $-4$. In summary 
   359		\begin{align*}
   360		S_{12}\cdot S_{23} \cdot \ldots \cdot S_{1n} &= \sum a_1\cdot \ldots \cdot a_n = \sum_{k=1}^n \binom{n}{k} 2\cdot 2^k -4 = 2((2+1)^n-1)-4 = 2\cdot 3^n-6 
   361	    \end{align*}
   362	    For $n=7$, this evaluates to $4368$.  
   363	\end{proof}
   364	
   365	\begin{remark}
   366		This can be also checked by an explicit computation in \texttt{OSCAR}, see \cite{code}.
   367	\end{remark} 
   368	
   369	In the setting of Proposition \ref{prop:reconstructionscorza}, Lemma \ref{lemma:scorzaclassmany} together with Lemma \ref{lemma:scorzabound} provides a bound of $4368$ for the number of isolated points in $T_7\cap J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$. This bound is too high, as we are aiming for $336$. However, we are also counting some points that do not belong to $J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$. To analyze this, we consider special configurations, which are called \enquote{biscribed triangles} in \cite{Mukai}:
   370	
   371	\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:scorzatriangles} 
   372	\begin{itemize}
   373		\item[(1)] On $C^3$, the set $T_3 = S_{12}\cap S_{23} \cap S_{13}$ consists of $48$ points, counted with multiplicity.
   374		\item[(2)] For any $[p_1,p_2,p_3]\in T_3/\mathfrak{S}_3$, there are $504$ configurations in $T_7\setminus J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$. In total, there are $4032$ such configurations in $T_7\setminus J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$.
   375	\end{itemize}
   376	\end{lemma}
   377	\begin{proof}
   378			(1) If the set $T_3$ is finite, then its number of points counted with multiplicity can be computed via the intersection product $S_{12}\cdot S_{23}\cdot S_{31} = 48$, where the last equality follows from Lemma \ref{lemma:scorzaclassmany}. This computation was already done by Mukai in \cite{Mukai}*{Proposition p.~4} to count the biscribed triangles to a plane quartic. We include here a proof that the set $T_3$ is indeed finite. If $(p_1,p_2,p_3)\in T_3$, a reasoning similar to that in Proposition \ref{prop:reconstructionscorza} shows that 
   379			\begin{align*}
   380			\eta+p_1-p_2 &\sim p_3+q_1, & \eta+p_2-p_1 &\sim p_3+q_2, \\
   381			\eta+p_2-p_3 &\sim p_1+q_2, & \eta+p_3-p_2 &\sim p_1+q_3, \\
   382			\eta+p_3-p_1 &\sim p_2+q_3, & \eta+p_1-p_3 &\sim p_2+q_1.
   383			\end{align*}
   384			If we sum the rows, we see that the lines $\ell(q_1,q_2),\ell(q_2,q_3),\ell(q_3,q_1)$ are tangent to $C$ at $p_3,p_1,p_2$ respectively. Hence the name biscribed triangles. To show that $T_3$ is finite, we will show that the projection $T_3\to C$ to each component has finite image, or, equivalently, that it is not surjective. By symmetry, it is enough to consider the projection onto the first component. We show that if $(p_1,p_2,p_3)\in T_3$, then $p_1$ cannot be a point such that its tangent line is bitangent to $C$. Assume by contradiction that $p_1$ is such a point, so that $2p_1+2p'_1 \sim H$ for some $p'_1 \in C$. But we know that $q_2+q_3+2p_1 \sim H$ as well, so that $q_2+q_3 \sim 2p'_1$. Since $C$ is not hyperelliptic, any two linearly equivalent effective divisors of degree $2$ are equal, implying $q_2=q_3=p'_1$. This shows $\eta+p_2-p_3 \sim \eta+p_3-p_2$, so that $2p_2\sim 2p_3$. The same reasoning as before shows $p_2=p_3$. But this is impossible, because then $h^0(C,\eta+p_2-p_3) = h^0(C,\eta)=0$.  
   385			
   386	        (2) Let $(p_1,p_2,p_3)\in T_3$. Following the reasoning in the previous point, we see that
   387			\begin{align*}
   388			\eta+p_1 \sim p_2+p_3+q_1, \quad \eta+p_2\sim p_1+p_3+q_2, \quad \eta+p_3\sim p_1+p_2+q_3
   389			\end{align*}
   390		    In particular, by Riemann-Roch, or by the symmetry of the Scorza correspondence, we see that $h^0(C,\eta+q_i-p_i)>0$ for $i=1,2,3$, so that
   391		    \begin{align*}
   392		    \eta+q_1 \sim a_1+b_1+p_1, \quad \eta+q_2 \sim a_2+b_2+p_2, \quad \eta+q_3 \sim a_3+b_3+p_3 
   393		    \end{align*}
   394	        for certain $a_i,b_j\in C$. Consider now the following graph:
   395	
   396	        \begin{center}
   397	        	\begin{tikzcd}[cramped,sep=small]
   398	        		&                         & a_3 \arrow[rd, no head] &                         & b_3 \arrow[ld, no head] &                         &                        \\
   399	        		&                         &                         & q_3 \arrow[d, no head]  &                         &                         &                        \\
   400	        		&                         &                         & p_3 \arrow[ld, no head] &                         &                         &                        \\
   401	        		&                         & p_1 \arrow[rr, no head] &                         & p_2 \arrow[lu, no head] &                         &                        \\
   402	        		a_1 \arrow[r, no head] & q_1 \arrow[ru, no head] &                         &                         &                         & q_2 \arrow[lu, no head] & a_2 \arrow[l, no head] \\
   403	        		& b_1 \arrow[u, no head]  &                         &                         &                         & b_2 \arrow[u, no head]  &                       
   404	        	\end{tikzcd}
   405	           \end{center}
   406	       We claim that if $x,y\in C$ are points connected by an edge in this graph, then $(x,y)
   407	       \in S(\eta)$. For example, for $q_1,b_1$ we see from the previous linear equivalences that $\eta+q_1-b_1 \sim a_1+p_1$, so that $(q_1,b_1)\in S(\eta)$. Since the Scorza correspondence is symmetric, it follows that $(b_1,q_1)\in S(\eta)$, as well. An analogous reasoning holds for all other edges. In particular, any closed walk of length $7$ along this graph, with vertices $y_1\to y_2\to y_3\to y_4 \to y_5 \to y_6 \to y_7 \to y_1$, yields a configuration $(y_1,y_2,\dots,y_7) \in T_7$. One can compute the number of such closed walks by writing down the $12\times 12$ adjacency matrix of the above graph and then taking the trace of its $7$-th power. This can be implemented on a computer, for example in \texttt{Julia}, and it yields $504$. Furthermore, any permutation of the elements $(p_1,p_2,p_3)\in T_3$ will induce a permutation in the set of closed walks in the graph, so that it gives the same set of configurations. This shows that for every point $(p_1,p_2,p_3)\in T_3$ there are $504/6$ such configurations in $T_7$, so that by (1) we get $48 \cdot 504/6 = 4032$ in total. To conclude, we need to show that these configurations do not belong to $J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$. This is simply because in any such configuration there are two identical points, so it cannot arise from a heptagon.
   408	\end{proof}
   409	
   410	\begin{corollary}\label{cor:conditionone}
   411		The number of isolated points in $T_7 \cap J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$ is bounded by $336$ and Statement $(*)$ in Lemma \ref{lemma:twoconditions} is true.
   412	\end{corollary}
   413	\begin{proof}
   414		Lemma \ref{lemma:scorzabound} and Lemma \ref{lemma:scorzaclassmany} show that the number of isolated points in $T_7$, counted with multiplicity, is bounded by $4368$. Furthermore, Lemma \ref{lemma:scorzatriangles} shows that amongst these isolated points there are $36\cdot 14\cdot 48/6 = 4032$ points that are contained in $T_7 \setminus J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$. Hence, the number of isolated points in $T_7 \cap J^{-1}(\mathcal{H})$ is bounded by $4368-4032 = 336$. At this point, Proposition \ref{prop:reconstructionscorza} implies Statement $(*)$ of Lemma \ref{lemma:twoconditions}.  
   415	\end{proof}
