422:This classical curve is very well studied, see e.g.~\cite{zbMATH01574981} for a survey. We will show that there is a theta characteristic $\etasym$ on $C$ such that $(C,\eta)$ are adjoint to $336$ heptagons, and that the map $\operatorname{adj}$ is unramified at each of these heptagons. We achieve this by using the large automorphism group $\operatorname{Aut}(C) \cong \mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb{F}_7)$ of $168$ elements. Notice that the automorphism group acts linearly on $C$, i.e.~as a subgroup of $\mathrm{PGL}_3(\mathbb{C})$. This is because the plane model of $C$ is precisely its canonical embedding.
